## Running a mission

1. **Make a notepad for the objective.** Tell Claude Code `/scope-init`. It creates a small git
   repo (`NOTES.md`, `repos.manifest.json`, a session journal) that keeps the objective's memory
   across sessions and drives the code repos from one place. Start every session for that
   objective in the notepad.
2. **Define the mission there.** Tell it `/dark-factory-build`, then one sentence of what done
   looks like: *"Mission: <what done looks like>. Chart the map, then run it to done. Notify me
   only when it is met or blocked."* Name anything it must never do (a production deploy, a merge
   to a protected branch, a message to a customer). `/vinculum-loop` explains the contract it runs
   under: it decides what it can, and comes back only when the mission is met or blocked.
3. **Work `operator-todo.md`.** Once a mission runs, `operator-todo.md` appears at the notepad
   root: the live page of what only a human can do, such as a login, a decision or a merge. The
   agent adds to it, you work it, and either of you updates it to unblock the mission. A finished
   item is deleted; git keeps the history. `df-operator-todo list` prints it.
4. **Unattended, if you want it.** `df-mission start <id> --profile default --max-iter <n>
   --max-usd <n>` runs the mission without a session open; `df-mission status <id>` shows it.

A team kit that binds an estate names that estate's mission skill here instead of
`/dark-factory-build`.

### One command instead of steps 1 and 2

`bash bin/link.sh` puts `df-start`, `df-open` and `df-bg` on your PATH. Then, from the repo you
are working in:

```sh
df-start "<one sentence of what done looks like>"
```

It builds the notepad (the `/scope-init` answers, taken from your sentence), attaches this kit's
hard stops from `<kit>/df-start.never`, finds the repo's end-to-end suites, and opens the mission
skill on it. Useful options: `--from <file>` for a pasted bug report or alert · `--e2e` to make
the suites required · `--bg` to run it in the background, then `df-bg attach <id>` to answer it ·
`--no-launch` to build the notepad and stop.

**If you hold more than one kit, give each its own names:** `bash bin/link.sh --prefix <estate>`
links `<estate>-df-start` and friends. The names must differ — `link.sh` refuses to overwrite one
another kit already took — and the prefix also selects the mission skill, so `acme-df-start`
opens `/acme-dark-factory`.

⚠️ **`--from` writes what you paste into the notepad.** Credential shapes are redacted where the
kit installs a redactor, and the file is kept out of git, but a redactor matches shapes and a
person's name has no shape. What you paste is yours to judge.
